            "text": "Compares two tensors element by element and gives back a tensor of true and false: true wherever a is not equal to b.\n\nThe answer is not one yes or no but a whole tensor of them, the same shape as the inputs, holding the outcome at every position. That tensor is a MASK - the way to say \"these elements, not those\" to everything that follows.\n\nCOMPARING FLOATS FOR EQUALITY:\nThis tests EXACT equality, and two floats that ought to be the same after arithmetic very often are not - 0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3. If it answers the opposite of what you are sure is right, that is usually why. Compare whole numbers (t.round first), or test the size of the difference against a small tolerance with t.lt instead.\n\nWHAT TO DO WITH A MASK:\nMultiply by it and everything that failed becomes zero. t.masked_select takes out just the values where it is true. t.count_nonzero (or t.sum) counts how many passed. To get the PROPORTION that passed, put t.to float32 in front of t.mean: torch will not average a bool tensor (t.mean on it raises 'could not infer output dtype'). Summing and multiplying work on it as it is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.ne\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.ne\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor a in:\nThe tensor to test (a list or number is turned into a float32 tensor). Receiving it makes the node compare and send.\n\ntensor b in:\nWhat to test against: another tensor, or a single number used for every element. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it FIRST; until it has received something the node sends nothing. It is converted to tensor a's type, so against an integer tensor a threshold of 4.5 becomes 4.\n\nThe shapes must broadcast: the same shape, a single number, or shapes that differ only where one has length 1 - so a column of thresholds tests each row against its own.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor result:\nA bool tensor, true where a != b, in the broadcast shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top strip is tensor a: the whole numbers 0 to 11. The strip under it is the mask - white (1) wherever the number differs from the slider. At 5 every cell is white except the one holding 5. Drag the slider and the dark cell moves along. The gradient holds exact whole numbers, which is why equality is meaningful here.\n\nRELATED:\nt.eq is the exact opposite. t.gt and t.lt split a range at a value. != does the same for numbers and NumPy arrays. np.where uses a mask to choose between two arrays."
        },
